%I A396112 #48 Aug 18 2026 22:16:16
%S A396112 1,3,4,8,13,19,24,30,39,47,54,66,77,91,100,116,133,147,160,180
%N A396112 Recamán's Choice. Take the integers 1..n and order them any way you choose to form a vector. Starting with the first element, add or subtract the numbers, keeping a running tally of the result. You must always use subtraction if you can, but the running tally can never be negative and can never duplicate a previous number in the running tally. a(n) is the largest possible final result of the running tally.
%C A396112 Vectors such as (4,1,2,3) in which an addition step produces a duplicate tally are disallowed.
%C A396112 I cannot prove that this sequence monotonically increases.
%C A396112 This sequence is excellent for students learning subtraction.
%C A396112 a(n) <= A000217(n). Also A000217(n) - a(n) is even. - _David A. Corneth_, Aug 11 2026
%H A396112 David A. Corneth, <a href="/A396112/a396112_1.gp.txt">PARI program</a>
%H A396112 David A. Corneth, <a href="/A396112/a396112.txt">Solutions for a(1)..a(17)</a>
%e A396112 a(8) is at least 24 because the vector {7,8,3,5,1,4,2,6} has a running tally of [7,15,12,17,16,20,18,24]. The 8 cannot be subtracted because 7-8 = -1, and we never allow the tally to be negative. The 3 must be subtracted because 12 has not previously been in the running tally. The 5 must be added because 12-5 = 7 has appeared in the running tally before, so we must instead try 12+5 = 17. That has not been on the running tally before, so we continue.
%e A396112 a(8) is actually 30 because of the vector {4,1,8,7,2,5,3,6} which produces a running tally of [4,3,11,18,16,21,24,30] discovered by _David A. Corneth_.
%o A396112 (PARI) \\ See Corneth link
%Y A396112 Cf. A000217, A005132 (Recamán's sequence).
%K A396112 nonn,more,new
%O A396112 1,2
